00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.772
And I hope that, throughout
this presentation,

00:01:09.772 --> 00:01:11.730
if you have any questions
feel free to put them

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:12.530
in the chat box.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.100
And we'll try to
address them at the end.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.030
And so for today's
talk, I'm hoping

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.620
to tell you a little bit
about what ontologies are

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.890
and why we should,
perhaps, be using them,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.940
a little bit about building
and reasoning over ontologies,

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.770
and then some applications
in biomedicine.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.470
So what is an ontology?

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.820
Well, ontology stands for
logical discourse of existence.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.840
And the real goal of ontology
is to uncover and describe

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.520
the nature and
structure of things.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.889
It's been, as Mark
said, predominantly

00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:46.930
the domain of philosophy.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.330
It's a very long history in
the field of metaphysics.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.000
And it's been associated
with prominent philosophers

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.384
such as Plato and Aristotle.

00:01:55.384 --> 00:01:57.590
Now, in the case
in Plato, he mostly

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.820
focused on what he
called Forms, which--

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.590
they were kind of conceptual.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.790
They could be of
any kind of thing

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.520
and not really are
necessarily tied to reality.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.570
Whereas Aristotle focused on
an empirical kind of analysis,

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.860
where the types of
things that we see

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.070
are really as a result
of what actually exists,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.350
what currently exists.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:26.130
So ontology, the
study of ontology,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.870
attempts to address
questions such as,

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.470
what does it mean to be?

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.660
What constitutes the
identity of an object?

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.700
And what categories can we
sort existing things into?

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.840
And as this field
has developed, I

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.510
think the principal idea is
that ontologies, particularly

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.270
when they're
communicated to others,

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.760
foster a shared understanding
of things, the nature of things,

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.900
and their structures
and relationships.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.734
So early bio-ontologists
do include Aristotle

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:03.310
in fourth century
BC-- third century

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:07.840
BC, where he developed-- made
many contributions to the field

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.770
of ontology and also provided
for systematic taxonomy

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.000
of biology.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.780
The classifications of
organisms were typically

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.360
by some shared properties and
used this binomial nomenclature

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.932
of that focused on
genus differentia.

00:03:21.932 --> 00:03:23.860
And I'll talk a
little bit about that.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.880
Galen is another, also
very famous, individual

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.170
that focused on
medical applications

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.380
and medical ontology for
the systematic description

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.610
of diseases, science,
and symptoms.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.891
And he also used this
Aristotelean genus-differential

00:03:40.891 --> 00:03:41.390
approach.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.000
So what is this?

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.420
So this approach is
really key, I think,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.820
to building good ontologies.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.940
It's a kind of
intentional definition

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.510
where you do provide the
necessary and sufficient

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.780
conditions in the definition.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.250
And you can see the definitions
are composed of two parts.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.110
The first is the
genus, which serves

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:00.910
as the basis for
a new definition.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.690
And basically, all
definitions that

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.240
contain this genus
component are considered

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.300
to be members of that genus.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.680
And then the other component
is some differentia,

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.200
something that tells you a
little bit more specifically

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.060
about what differentiates
this type of thing

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.560
from any other type.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.110
So, for instance, if you have,
say, a type like a rhombus,

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:22.910
you can define it
as a quadrilateral.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:25.092
So the quadrilateral
part is the genus.

00:04:25.092 --> 00:04:27.050
And of course, that would
be defined elsewhere.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.220
But you're using it as a basis
to form your own definition

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.100
here.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.346
And the particulars
of the rhombus

00:04:33.346 --> 00:04:37.000
is that it has bounding sides
which all have the same length.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.450
And so we can build on
that to define a square.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:40.880
We can say a square
is a rhombus.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.900
So it inherits the properties
that we've described above.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:49.840
And it also has interior angles
which are all right angles.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.770
When we build
ontologies in this way,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.480
we can navigate it almost
like a binary decision tree.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.100
You can look at the
properties and see if it fits

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.610
and continue down that
branch or perhaps go down

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.160
a different branch
based on that structure.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.360
So, for instance, for
Aristotle's categories,

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.100
the main object type
was substance-- or one

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.720
of the more important
ones was substance.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.450
And then you had a
differentia, which

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.990
would be, for instance,
whether the object was

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.670
material or immaterial.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:22.060
If it was immaterial,
it was kind of spirit.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.320
If it was material,
it was a kind of body.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.200
And then you had two
other differentia,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.855
where you would look whether
the body was inanimate,

00:05:29.855 --> 00:05:32.390
in which case it would
be a kind of mineral.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:34.269
And if it was animate,
it would be living.

00:05:34.269 --> 00:05:36.310
And similarly, for living,
you would look-- is it

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.190
sensitive or insensitive?

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.290
And if it was sensitive,
it was an animal.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:41.830
And then, from an
animal, you look

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:43.960
at whether it was
rational or irrational.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.450
And that would
lead you eventually

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.320
to the category of
human-- and then

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.750
from which individuals
that exist in the world

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.600
would instantiate this
category and would

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.820
basically-- we would
infer that humans

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:01.266
were rational, sensitive,
animate and material

00:06:01.266 --> 00:06:01.766
substances.

00:06:01.766 --> 00:06:07.880
 

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.930
Biologists have a long history
of trying to do classification.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:15.960
And of course, this
example is no better known

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.430
than in biological
taxonomy, where

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.120
we have different
kinds of categories

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.710
to describe the different
living organisms that we

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:24.934
see in the world today.

00:06:24.934 --> 00:06:27.840
 

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.170
One of the three main kingdoms
is animals, for instance.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:35.360
And these are divided into
phylum, into classes, orders,

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.130
families, genus, and species.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.010
While there are
other different kinds

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:43.510
of ranks for the classification,
these are the main ones.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:47.080
And you can see that, at
least in this diagram, how

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.500
it is simply articulated,
the differentia,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:55.010
to go from the parent categories
into more specific categories.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.205
So, for instance,
for animals, there

00:06:57.205 --> 00:06:59.760
are organisms that are
able to move on their own.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.380
Chordates are animals
with a backbone.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.860
Mammals are chordates with
fur or hair and no clans,

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.630
and so on, all the way
up to Homo sapiens.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:13.540
So this basically instantiates
a kind of taxonomy

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:17.450
where we have a
hierarchy of inheritance,

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:19.350
where you have an
"is a" relationship,

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.810
that every instance
of a chordate

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.610
is also an instance of
an animal, and so on.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.600
So you can really
see this structure,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.980
this taxonomic structure,
where you have an "is a"

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.720
relationship that holds
between one set of objects

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.610
and another set of objects.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.190
And so you can see on the
right-hand side, this kind

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:43.130
of illustration that
the entities that

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.000
are considered to
be mammals are also

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.290
considered to be chordates.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:51.090
The chordates are not only--
don't only necessarily contain

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:54.380
mammals, but could contain
other kinds of species.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.800
And similar with
animals, animals--

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.830
chordates are kinds of animals.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:01.853
But then there could also be
other kinds of animals present.

00:08:01.853 --> 00:08:06.460
 

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:13.640
So moving from this early
understanding or development

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.640
of ontology, there's been
this increasing trend to,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.370
I guess, in some sense,
operationalize ontology

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:26.220
to make it-- to apply it
into computational contexts.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.770
And this has been, as
Mark said, an evolution

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:34.380
over the past 30, 40, 50 years,
where people have been thinking

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.900
a little bit more
about what does it

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.100
mean to apply these kinds of
categorical systems to things,

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.780
and how do we use them?

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:48.180
So Tom Gruber is also a
very important individual

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.100
in this context.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:52.450
He was one of the developers
or inventors of the Siri

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.060
application, which you
can use on the iPhone.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.660
And he put forward
that an ontology

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.290
was an explicit specification
of a conceptualization.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:02.960
So let's break that down.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.950
So conceptualization
is the way that we

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.760
think about a domain,
something that we

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.320
can articulate to others.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:14.040
And a specification is some
way of writing that down.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.630
Borst, in '97, extended this
to say, so let's focus on that.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.880
And let's say, a formal
specification-- and again,

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:25.460
formal in the sense that you
have a formal language which

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.930
you can use to describe
the specification

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.710
in an unambiguous way.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:31.240
And he also specified
that it should

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.000
be a shared conceptualization.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.950
So it's not sufficient
that you just articulate it

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.680
with a formal language, but that
the definitions and meanings

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.460
that you have are shared
amongst different groups.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.700
And then Gruber came
back a little bit later.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.380
And again, we have a much
more pragmatic approach

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:52.990
to the description
of ontology, where

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:56.280
he says the ontology specifies
a vocabulary with which

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.400
to make assertions which may be
inputs and outputs of knowledge

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.840
agents, for instance,
a software program.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.620
But ultimately,
that ontology must

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.840
be formulated in some
representational language.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.770
 

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:13.190
So these kinds of requirements
are a little bit different

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:16.100
than what we might see for
other kinds of classification

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:16.920
systems.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:18.880
For instance, in a
folksonomy, which

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.470
we're very accustomed to-- when
we're tagging, for instance,

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:25.260
images or making
adaptations of some kind,

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.590
we use some collection of terms
to enhance overall ability

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:32.430
to navigate through
different kinds of content.

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:36.530
But it's not necessarily
really formally organized.

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:39.520
It's mostly loosely
coupled with our intuition

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:40.940
about what those terms mean.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:44.930
And in some cases,
people can use synonyms

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.510
for exactly the same concepts.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.650
So there's not much
constraint here.

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:52.410
In contrast-- so
a glossary really

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.860
provides both the terms
and some definition

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.350
and potential explanations
or examples of use.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:00.500
Controlled vocabulary
would focus more

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:04.040
on some enumeration of
terms that are defined so

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:07.030
that they can specifically
be shared and reused

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.900
and then so
communities would adopt

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.930
some particular set of terms
and ensure that these are well

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:15.580
understood by members
of the community

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:19.410
and used in a coherent manner.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.510
And then going back to
hierarchies-- again,

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.240
are just a nested set of terms.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:28.120
But the relation between one
term and another can vary.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.340
So, for instance, in
a proper taxonomy,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.910
you would expect to use
this "is a" relationship.

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:40.370
So every mammal is
a kind of animal.

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:42.400
But in a meronomy,
then you're really--

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.360
this relationship is a "part
of" relationship, right?

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:47.240
So you would have,
for instance, the arm

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:52.680
would be part of the body,
or something like that.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:56.230
And finally a classification,
which ontologies typically are

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.680
is a set of categories in
which objects are grouped

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.720
into what you can sort,
for instance, data into,

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.330
or any other thing.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.190
So why do we want to
develop an ontology?

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:11.620
Well, primarily, there are a
few different main-use cases.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.990
The first is to provide
a formal specification

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:15.480
of biomedical
knowledge something

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.530
that we can represent in a way
that is relatively unambiguous,

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:23.800
that both people and agents can
understand and consume and use

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:25.680
in some way.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:29.290
And one of those ways is that
they can do this classification

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:31.320
task by looking
at the definitions

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.430
or looking at the differentia
and trying to apply them

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.220
to, for instance, data.

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:39.600
We use them to develop
a common understanding

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:41.500
of the kinds of
entities in a given

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:44.660
domain and the relationships
that exist between them.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:48.610
And we use them to enable
reuse of data and knowledge

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.490
in different contexts
and ultimately,

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:54.590
in our case, for many of
us in this community that

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.460
are listening to
this webinar, is

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:02.050
we try to use ontologies to
enable biomedical discovery.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:05.620
So one of the most famous
or well-known ontologies

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:08.220
in the biomedical domain
is the gene ontology.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:10.580
There are millions of
annotations on hundreds

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:14.400
of thousands of genes that
use the gene ontology terms.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.680
The gene ontology
defines types that are

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.640
used to describe gene function.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:23.470
And it basically has three
main categorical aspects.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.185
The first is
molecular functions.

00:13:25.185 --> 00:13:26.640
So what do gene products do?

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.990
The second is
cellular component,

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:31.360
which is where are
the gene projects--

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.410
where do they operate?

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:36.119
And finally, there's a
biological process branch,

00:13:36.119 --> 00:13:38.035
which describes the
pathways and the processes

00:13:38.035 --> 00:13:40.650
that gene products
participate in.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.210
And so, on the
right hand side, you

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:46.290
can see-- this is a snapshot
from the AmiGO tool of the gene

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:47.370
ontology.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:50.517
It has this nested
hierarchy following the "is

00:13:50.517 --> 00:13:57.270
a" relationships, as denoted
with the [? "I." ?] And you can

00:13:57.270 --> 00:14:00.480
see-- so from biological
process branch,

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:03.020
one of the subtypes is behavior.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:07.200
And you can see next to it,
the numeric label indicates

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.570
the number of gene
products that have

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:15.130
been annotated with that label,
with that biological process.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:17.410
So as you navigate
further down the tree,

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.140
there will be a narrowing of
the total number of instances

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:21.350
that can be applied.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:24.760
But in total, there are
800,000 at least reported

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:27.556
on the AmiGO website.

00:14:27.556 --> 00:14:30.510
So what's interesting
about the gene ontology is

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:35.660
it's-- in essence, it was
formulated to handle a problem

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:40.080
where many different biological
databases were crafting

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.870
their own terms to annotate
to genes and provide them with

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:45.520
a functional description.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.890
And so the Gene
Ontology Consortium

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:51.610
came together to establish a
common vocabulary that could be

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.550
used independently of species.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:58.260
And so, on the bottom chart,
you can see the distribution

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:00.880
of annotations across
different species,

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:04.220
for instance, from
human and mouse and rat.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:07.525
And in that chart, it also
shows the distribution

00:15:07.525 --> 00:15:10.250
in terms of where
those annotations have

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:14.765
experimental evidence or
non-experimental evidence.

00:15:14.765 --> 00:15:16.970
So the vast majority
are somewhat

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.760
imputed from
computational means,

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:26.870
but human proteins and mice
proteins are very well studied.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:29.530
Now, in preparation
for this talk,

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.470
I also looked up
one of the proteins

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.750
that I had worked on very
early in my career, which

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:39.260
was on RACA-- 1A GTPase.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:42.280
And so, in UniProt-- that's
one of our protein databases--

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:45.050
also includes gene
ontology annotations

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:46.740
for protein products.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:50.270
And in our work we looked
at, through mutational

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.240
or perturbations,
we looked at what

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:55.010
RAC1 GTPases could be doing.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.720
And we saw that they
apparent-- they controlled

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:01.495
filopodia formation and
cell motility, endocytosis,

00:16:01.495 --> 00:16:04.760
cytokinesis, and
development in slime molds.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:08.190
And so these descriptions,
these functional descriptions,

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.250
are captured using
gene ontology terms.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:13.810
 

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:16.950
And, as I mentioned, so
what for-- certainly,

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:21.505
in the case here for RAC1A,
it's basically a protein kinase

00:16:21.505 --> 00:16:24.880
that has GTP-binding activity.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.670
And so, if we want to see what
are all the proteins that have

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:30.880
GTP binding, we can do that.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:33.570
And we can see there's a
wide distribution across all

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:34.980
the different species.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.760
So this is the big
win for an ontology

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:42.160
like the gene ontology-- is to
see that no matter what kind

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:44.500
of biological
organism you are, you

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.800
can use the same--
the terms that

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.520
are defined in a
consistent manner

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:54.380
in your own biological
annotation projects.

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:56.820
Now, clearly, the gene
ontology on its own

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:01.070
is a necessary development
in the description

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:02.060
of gene products.

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.930
But we also need
similar terminologies

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:07.930
across the biological
scales to describe

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.200
the different kinds of
proteins, the different kinds

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:12.630
of organelles, the different
kinds of cells and tissues

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:15.390
and organs and organisms
all together as we

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:19.099
saw with the natural taxonomy.

00:17:19.099 --> 00:17:22.329
So some disease and
phenotypes ontologies--

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:27.450
there are key health
care terminologies.

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:31.010
These are provided through
the UMLS system, where

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:33.900
we have terminologies
like SNOMED-CT

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:36.460
for clinical terminology
diseases, diagnostics,

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.260
and procedures; the
NCI Thesaurus, which

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.960
is a pretty wide-ranging
vocabulary for clinical care,

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:46.080
translational basic
research, public information

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:50.360
and administration activities;
LOINC for labs and vital signs;

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:54.100
and ICD-9 and 10 for disease,
epidemiology, and billing.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:57.610
 

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:01.070
Other efforts have also
complemented these ones

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:04.790
in the Open Biomedical
Ontology Consortium,

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:07.000
including the disease
ontology, which

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.770
offers a standardized
ontology for human disease.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:13.850
These are mapped to
these UMLS terminologies.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.870
And it's complemented with the
human phenotype ontology that

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:20.420
provides phenotypic descriptions
for human hereditary and now

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:22.750
many other diseases.

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:26.240
And Mammalian Phenotype Ontology
complements the human one

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.480
by being a little bit more
broad than just human phenotypes

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:32.400
and encompassing-- and
had been widely used

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:37.180
in different kinds of
phenotyping experiments,

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:40.470
for instance, for
mice, rats and so on.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.780
And finally, an
Experimental Factor Ontology

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.880
from the European
Bioinformatics Institute,

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:48.000
which takes in all of these
different kinds of ontologies

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.980
and tries to provide
support for discovery

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:58.680
in the context of many different
kinds of experimental datasets.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.020
So we can you go
and get ontologies?

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:06.770
Well, Mark Musen is the
PI for the BioPortal.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:11.080
It currently contains
517 biomedical ontologies

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.900
and over 7.8 million classes.

00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:19.590
So it's obviously an enormous
number of ontologies.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:22.932
It's a great resource for
biomedical researchers.

00:19:22.932 --> 00:19:25.260
It allows you to search
these UMLS ontologies

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.930
as well the opened by medical
technologies and many more.

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:33.710
There are other repositories
of ontologies and vocabularies.

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:36.510
One of these is the linked
open vocabularies effort.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:39.960
This is really largely
focused around ontologies

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:43.860
and terminologies to describe
data using semantic web

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:47.110
standards, for instance, a
research description framework,

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:49.490
but are widely used
outside of that as well.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:54.230
 

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:56.030
All right, so what
I'd like to do now

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:59.620
is just give you a quick
introduction, I guess,

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:04.310
to more of the idea
of how do we formalize

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:05.860
our conceptualizations,
how do we

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:10.320
build computable ontologies
and reason with them,

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.980
and then follow up
with applications.

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:17.620
So this-- again, this idea of
formalization is the process

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:20.390
by which we map our
conceptualization--

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:23.330
so what are the kinds
of entities that exist

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:25.090
in the domain; what
are the relationships

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:27.460
and their attributes?--
into some kind of logical

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:28.940
representation.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:33.720
And we can kind of see that
our logical representations are

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:35.590
essentially a set
of building blocks

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:38.990
in which we can
combine them to create

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.800
machine-understandable
expression that have

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:44.240
an unambiguous interpretation.

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:46.590
And therefore, we
can automatically

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:48.720
reason about them.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.870
So again, the idea is
that we have concepts

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:54.030
that we can reuse in
different contexts

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:55.735
and create new expressions.

00:20:55.735 --> 00:20:59.850
So, for instance,
description logics

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:04.980
is one of these languages that
we can use to build ontologies.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:07.060
And we can define
something, for instance,

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:11.420
like a transcription factor as
being a kind of protein that

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:15.480
binds to DNA molecules
and regulates

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:16.910
the rate of transcription.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:18.620
So this is an
expression that we can

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.610
describe in description logics.

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:25.630
And therefore, we can ask
questions about things

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:28.430
that bind to DNA or
things that regulate

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.290
the rate of transcription.

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:33.040
And we would discover that
transcription factors satisfy

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:33.540
these.

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:36.600
 

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.310
So one of the
languages that we have

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:43.140
available to build
ontologies is the OWL,

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.940
which stands for the
Web Ontology Language.

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:49.250
It is a product of the
Worldwide Web Consortium

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:52.750
and has gone through a couple
of different iterations now.

00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:55.940
And it's the de facto
standard for building computer

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:58.320
ontologies on the semantic web.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:01.470
There are many other languages
that are available, of course,

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:04.640
with all kinds of
interesting properties

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:09.070
and which may be better suited
for particular projects.

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:11.610
But this is the one
that I'll focus on here.

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:14.860
So one of the challenges of,
again, this formalization

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:18.650
process is now you really
have to think about what

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:21.500
you are saying to a machine.

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:24.430
Now it matters how
you describe something

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:30.150
because the interpretation
may be erroneous to us

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:33.840
if we don't correctly capture
it in the first place.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:36.940
So OWL specifies a
vocabulary and a grammar

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:40.640
to express more precisely what
we mean when we are describing

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:42.590
entities of interest.

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:45.900
It provides us with the
ability to articulate

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:49.130
different kinds of things
or classes, the relations

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:53.520
or properties, and to describe
even data so individuals

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:55.980
and their specific values.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:59.440
So different kinds of aspects
of the language, briefly--

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:01.910
disjointness, where
we want to know

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:06.330
whether things are the same
or different from one another.

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:08.520
We can quantify
our relationships.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:12.190
So we can say, for
instance, that if we wanted

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.440
to define the class
of parents, we

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:18.310
would expect that a parent
would have at least one child.

00:23:18.310 --> 00:23:23.120
And so we have this range
that we can quantify over

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:25.000
some relationship.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:28.560
We have vocabulary
for negation, where

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.590
we can say something is-- it's
not the case, or, for instance,

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:34.480
that we can say that
penguins are kinds of birds,

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:37.900
but they do not
fly, for instance.

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:41.090
We also have aspects that
relate to disjunction,

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:42.950
so basically the
ability to express

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:46.230
a union, and a variety
of different property

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:48.200
characteristics for
automated reasoning,

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:51.030
so, for instance,
for transitivity,

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:54.730
for inferring
functional properties

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:57.540
or inverse functional properties
and symmetric properties,

00:23:57.540 --> 00:23:58.380
and so on.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:00.200
And there's a few
other aspects that

00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:04.220
are-- like complex cause
expressions and property

00:24:04.220 --> 00:24:08.915
chains-- that are very useful
but are probably more seldomly

00:24:08.915 --> 00:24:09.415
used.

00:24:09.415 --> 00:24:12.070
 

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:15.210
So what do we get with
reasoning over OWL ontologies?

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.900
Well, there's a number
of different properties

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:22.680
of-- a number of different
aspects that we can use.

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.310
The first is
consistency, where we

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:26.420
can determine whether
an ontology contains

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.346
contradictions.

00:24:28.346 --> 00:24:29.720
There is
satisfiability, where we

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:32.840
can determine whether
classes can have instances

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:36.930
or whether they are logically
basically unsatisfiable,

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:41.580
that no instances could ever
fulfill the description that's

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.760
provided because it's
logically inconsistent.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:47.650
We can compute subsumption,
where we're asking,

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:51.247
are instances of one class also
instances of another class?

00:24:51.247 --> 00:24:54.890
Do they satisfy those
minimal requirements?

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:58.790
We can perform classification,
where we repeatedly

00:24:58.790 --> 00:25:03.920
apply subsumption to discover
links between name classes.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:05.715
And finally, we can
perform realization,

00:25:05.715 --> 00:25:09.470
where, if we have individuals
in our knowledge base,

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:11.944
we can see if they are
members of classes that

00:25:11.944 --> 00:25:13.110
are defined in the ontology.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:16.280
 

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:19.300
So we can use
tools like Protege,

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:23.700
which is also developed by Mark,
to construct our ontologies.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:27.340
 

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:31.180
It implements the OWL standard.

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:32.736
And it's extensible.

00:25:32.736 --> 00:25:34.110
People have been
writing plugins.

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:37.140
So there's a wonderful
software framework

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:40.010
to build on and
exploit ontologies

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:41.980
that are being built.

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:44.460
I show a screenshot
on the lower-hand side

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:47.860
where, again, I designed a
quick and dirty ontology just

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:53.220
to describe that proteins have
amino acids as their part.

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:55.440
And they're are also
types of molecules.

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:58.730
So this can be done very
quickly, very readily.

00:25:58.730 --> 00:26:01.010
There are also other
products on the market

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:03.060
to build ontologies,
including, for instance,

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:07.640
TopBraid Composer, which offers
this graphical interface.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:11.940
But they both share
this very common view

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:15.250
where you have this tree-like
view of the different classes

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:18.200
and a description of their
properties, the attributes.

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:22.314
 

00:26:22.314 --> 00:26:26.990
Right, so just a quick focus
here on the Protege Editor.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:29.490
You can see this is the layout.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.760
So we have, on the upper
left-hand side, the class

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:35.080
hierarchy organic molecule.

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:37.610
And we have proteins that
are types, sub-classes

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.650
of organic molecules and amino
acids that are also sub-classes

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.280
of organic molecules.

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:46.710
And in the lower left panel,
you can see a property

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:49.720
that we have defined,
which is the "has" part.

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:52.880
And so this-- now we
have our terminology

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:56.500
and our domain, which is the
different kinds of objects

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:59.120
that we're interested in--
organic molecules, proteins,

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:01.630
amino acids-- and
a relation to help

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:05.800
relate these two-- any of
the two or more entities

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:07.950
of interest, which
is "has" part.

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:11.230
So in the lower
right-hand side, you

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:14.200
can craft these
logical expressions

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:15.890
to describe the
relationships that

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:17.195
exist between these entities.

00:27:17.195 --> 00:27:20.990
So, for instance, now, we're
focused on the protein entity.

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:23.910
We have two sub-class axioms.

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:27.380
The first is that-- so we
have that every protein

00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:29.580
is a kind of organic molecule.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:33.100
And so that forms the
genus of our definition.

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:38.230
And then secondly, that
proteins are comprised-- or have

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:41.860
amino acids "as part." so
that's our differentia.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:45.610
And so the Protege editor offers
this auto-complete service

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:51.777
that helps you craft these OWL--
these syntactically correct OWL

00:27:51.777 --> 00:27:52.276
expressions.

00:27:52.276 --> 00:27:56.940
 

00:27:56.940 --> 00:28:00.710
So, again, this is easy
enough for everybody

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:03.680
to do to go to the Protege
website, to download the tool,

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:07.940
to start playing around with
ontologies and reasonings.

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:09.670
One of the ontologies
that we have built

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:13.010
is the Semantic Science
Integrated Ontology, or SSIO.

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:16.900
And it counters many of the
different basic kinds of things

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:19.420
in the domain and
divides up the world

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:24.290
into three aspects, objects, and
processes and their attributes.

00:28:24.290 --> 00:28:28.720
And then we can create even
more sophisticated expressions

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:32.650
for each of the basic biomedical
data types of interest.

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:36.440
So here I show you
a hierarchy that

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:41.940
relates to-- it goes all
the way down to a protein.

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:44.160
And there are other
ontologies out there

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:46.690
that provide
descriptions of proteins

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:48.790
and other kinds of biomolecules.

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:52.910
But suffice it to say
that, because we have also

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:55.770
added these axioms
into the ontology,

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:58.450
we can ask questions
that are not just

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:02.120
about to retrieve the label,
like, use the labels of things

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:05.980
to retrieve the types
of things of interest.

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:07.460
But we can actually
use the axiom.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:10.070
We can ask whether or
not, for instance, what

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:13.820
are all the things that have
amino acid residues as a part?

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:15.740
And we get protein,
of course, as one

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:19.490
of our answers, polypeptides,
enzymes, antibodies, protein

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:20.650
complexes.

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:26.770
And amino acid residues are
themselves, by definition,

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:28.450
are holding themselves.

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:32.500
So you also see another term
there, which is all-nothing.

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:34.660
And the Protege
Editor, by default,

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:39.390
shows you that the sum
of all things is nothing.

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:45.690
So it's just an artifact
of the language.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:49.140
OK, so in the last
bit, what I hope to do

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:52.235
is talk to you about a
couple different applications

00:29:52.235 --> 00:29:56.470
in biomedicine to give you a
sense of what we and others are

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:58.610
doing in this domain.

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:00.925
And by no means is
this comprehensive.

00:30:00.925 --> 00:30:03.080
There is a vast body of work.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:07.630
And hopefully you'll see more
of it in the seminar series.

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:10.000
But here's a few
different applications

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:13.000
that I want to bring
to your attention.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.600
So SNOMED-CT is one of
these ontologies that are

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:18.350
used in the health-care domain.

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:21.310
It's used across many
different countries

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:24.650
and by many providers
in the United States.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:27.670
It provides common vocabulary
for recording clinical data.

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:31.080
And it's comprised of
about 324,000 classes.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.442
So it's a huge ontology.

00:30:33.442 --> 00:30:34.900
And on the right-hand
side, you can

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:41.202
see a sort of snapshot
of part of SNOMED-CT

00:30:41.202 --> 00:30:42.910
towards the upper
[? lateral. ?] So there

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:46.240
are body structures,
clinical findings, organisms,

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:48.430
pharmaceuticals,
procedures, and so on.

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:51.120
Lots of different
things in there.

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:54.106
Now, SNOMED-CT uses a
pattern-based knowledge

00:30:54.106 --> 00:30:54.605
capture.

00:30:54.605 --> 00:30:58.100
So they really want
to express again

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:03.490
the features of each of the
categories that you see there.

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:08.140
But you can see the expression
is very difficult to parse.

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:10.940
You have to pretty much
be trained in this.

00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:14.600
But the idea is that, once
you adopt a kind of pattern

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:18.790
for defining your genus
or your differentia,

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:22.110
then you can actually
automatically construct

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:24.190
and validate the ontology.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:27.180
So we'll show you
an example here,

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:32.300
where Kaiser Permanente had done
some work to extend SNOMED-CT

00:31:32.300 --> 00:31:34.720
to express some different
kinds of categories,

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:38.340
in particular, non-viral
pneumonia, in which, again,

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:41.300
here, this term contains an
element of negation-- you're

00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:44.670
looking for things that are
not viral-- and infectious

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:47.710
pneumonia, which would be
caused by a virus or bacterium--

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:51.000
so here you have a disjunctive
clause-- and double pneumonia,

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.320
which occurs in two ones.

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:56.590
So you're using this
cardinality description.

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:59.950
In an implementation of
this, called SNOMED-OWL,

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:05.190
you can express these
kinds of definitions.

00:32:05.190 --> 00:32:08.850
And when you use an
automated reasoner, what's

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:11.540
interesting is that it failed
to find some of the expected

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:13.750
subsumptions, for instance,
that bacterial pneumonia

00:32:13.750 --> 00:32:17.150
is a kind of
non-viral pneumonia.

00:32:17.150 --> 00:32:19.030
And part of the reason
is that the ontology

00:32:19.030 --> 00:32:20.200
was under-constrained.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:23.120
It didn't know, the ontology--
we didn't provide the reason

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:26.440
or information that virus
and bacterium are not

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:29.670
the same things,
which are required

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:31.840
to make the entailment.

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:36.610
But adding disjointness
in these concepts

00:32:36.610 --> 00:32:40.500
led to other results from the
reasoner, and, for instance,

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:43.560
that one of the classes
that became inconsistent

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:47.120
is this percutaneous
embolisation hepatic artery

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:50.330
using fluoroscopy guidance.

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:52.500
And the cause of
this inconsistency,

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:55.880
which you can learn by asking
the reasoner to provide you

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:58.830
with some justification
for the inconsistency,

00:32:58.830 --> 00:33:02.300
is that there is a problem in
the description of groin, which

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:05.580
was asserted to be a sub-class
of both an abdomen and the leg.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:08.870
And these were-- these are
disjoint in the ontology.

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:11.460
And so this results
in the total,

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:14.790
like, a huge amount of
inconsistency in the ontology.

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:17.420
So the nice thing
about using a language

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:20.240
like OWL-- a formal language,
along with the reasoner--

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:21.880
is that when you--
again, when you're

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:23.760
making these kinds of
statements, when you're

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:26.310
providing the
descriptions that at least

00:33:26.310 --> 00:33:29.680
provide necessary conditions
if not sufficient conditions,

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:33.950
it can find those expressions
that are contradictory

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:34.864
to one another.

00:33:34.864 --> 00:33:39.510
 

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:42.680
Another increasingly-- I mean,
just like we saw with the gene

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:46.240
ontology, where we were using
an ontology for annotation

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:50.020
of genes, we also
use, for instance,

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:53.360
phenotype ontologies to do
deep clinical phenotyping

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:54.460
of patients.

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:57.110
So here, this is one
tool called Phenotips,

00:33:57.110 --> 00:33:59.280
which uses the human
phenotype ontology

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:02.450
to provide descriptions
of-- on human patients.

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:05.890
And so it provides you
the ability to navigate,

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:09.840
basically-- an easier navigation
of the ontology itself,

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:13.760
but the ability to say that, for
instance, a patient has or does

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:20.659
not have a specific signs or
symptoms of some disorder,

00:34:20.659 --> 00:34:23.400
and provides a
computable representation

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:26.449
of this description.

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:29.570
This kind of information,
this deep clinical phenotyping

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:33.500
using ontologies, can be used
to match patients together.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:37.610
So PhenomeCentral is one
of these tools-- portals

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:39.310
that can be used
to find patients

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:41.780
that match the one
that you're looking at

00:34:41.780 --> 00:34:43.679
or that you characterized.

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:46.510
So you'll have a set of
phenotypes for your patient.

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:49.030
And through measures
of semantic similarity,

00:34:49.030 --> 00:34:50.570
you're going to try
to find patients

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:52.150
that are most like this one.

00:34:52.150 --> 00:34:55.510
And in this tool, because
it's focused on rare diseases,

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:57.680
the idea is to find
other patients that share

00:34:57.680 --> 00:34:59.360
similar phenotypes
and potentially

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:03.380
share the same disease etiology.

00:35:03.380 --> 00:35:06.690
And so there's the
opportunity to communicate

00:35:06.690 --> 00:35:13.560
with other providers that know
something about these patients.

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:17.750
Another application here of
using phenotypic ontologies

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:22.080
or disease ontologies is to
pinpoint-- more accurately

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:25.000
pinpoint the genetic
basis for disorders.

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:30.550
So in this work here
for Phenix, where,

00:35:30.550 --> 00:35:33.765
once you collect genomic
information about the patient,

00:35:33.765 --> 00:35:36.400
coupled with this deep,
clinical phenotyping

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:41.250
and modeling it, or looking at
the different disorders that

00:35:41.250 --> 00:35:44.120
have been described
in, for instance,

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:47.080
the Orphanet or
OMIM or ClinVar, it

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:51.420
becomes possible to prioritize
the set of genes that come up

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:54.010
from some variant
analysis, coupled

00:35:54.010 --> 00:35:56.910
with the phenotypic profile
to identify the genes that

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:59.650
are most likely to be
responsible for eliciting

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:02.070
the phenotypes that we observe.

00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:06.060
So this has been applied and has
helped to identify individuals

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:09.640
with fairly rare disorders
that occur very infrequently.

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:11.710
But the fact that we
have these descriptions

00:36:11.710 --> 00:36:15.010
allow us to identify
candidate genes.

00:36:15.010 --> 00:36:17.784
 

00:36:17.784 --> 00:36:19.450
All right, so finally,
what I want to do

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:21.700
is just tell you about
another-- some of the work

00:36:21.700 --> 00:36:24.480
that we have been
doing, where we use

00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:27.330
ontologies for drug discovery.

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:29.490
So one of the
projects that we did

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:33.950
was to identify human drug
targets using the phenotypes

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:41.150
from knockout mice models
and basically the side

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:43.430
effects of known drugs.

00:36:43.430 --> 00:36:45.940
And so the main idea
here is that if we

00:36:45.940 --> 00:36:49.310
can compare the phenotypes
of knockout mouse models

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:52.880
with the effects of drugs,
that when they are similar,

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:54.950
it would suggest
that the drug might

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:58.460
be an inhibitor of that
gene which produces

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:01.730
that phenotypic profile.

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:03.750
So to do this kind
of experiment,

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:08.400
though, the problem is that
drugs and their effects

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:14.130
are expressed using MEDRA
or UMLS terminology.

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:17.440
This is from one of the
databases called SIGIR, which

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:21.750
basically does text mining
using ontologies over products

00:37:21.750 --> 00:37:23.660
structure or product labels.

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:29.530
And we also have phenotypes
from the [INAUDIBLE] genome

00:37:29.530 --> 00:37:33.540
database, which are
expressed using the Mammalian

00:37:33.540 --> 00:37:34.770
phenotype ontology.

00:37:34.770 --> 00:37:38.540
So we basically have now
the problem that we have,

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:39.740
like, apples and oranges.

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:42.030
On the one hand, we have
a million phenotypes.

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:44.550
And then, on the other
hand, we have drug effects.

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:46.300
But they're not using
the same vocabulary.

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:48.240
So it's hard to
do the comparison

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:50.750
between, for instance,
a "drug side effect"

00:37:50.750 --> 00:37:59.030
profile and a mouse
model phenotypic profile.

00:37:59.030 --> 00:38:00.780
So what we have to
do at that point

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:02.770
is create mappings
or a correspondence

00:38:02.770 --> 00:38:04.470
between the different
terminologies.

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:07.400
And this is really what
mappings are used for

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:10.250
to establish a
correspondence between one

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:13.090
term and another term from
different vocabularies.

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:15.850
And so this allows us
to have a common-- when

00:38:15.850 --> 00:38:18.045
we can map one set
of terms to another,

00:38:18.045 --> 00:38:21.540
it allows us then to compare
apples with apples again.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:25.430
And so we use measures of what
we call semantic similarity

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:28.550
to compare a list of, for
instance, drug effects

00:38:28.550 --> 00:38:32.280
with a list of mice
model phenotypes.

00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:35.490
And then we can, looking
at their-- the intersection

00:38:35.490 --> 00:38:37.760
over their union, we
can assign a score

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:43.490
that says just how similar those
profiles are to one another.

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:45.810
So when we do that,
when we compute

00:38:45.810 --> 00:38:50.350
the similarity between
drugs and their effects

00:38:50.350 --> 00:38:54.290
and mice models and their
phenotypes, what we find

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:58.690
is that we can, in fact,
recover drug targets in this way

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:03.340
pretty accurately-- in a way
that's fairly surprising.

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:05.640
That phenotypic
information, information

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:11.090
about the effects of
knocking out genes in mice,

00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:14.650
often look like the effects
that we see in drugs

00:39:14.650 --> 00:39:16.710
as well that target those genes.

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:21.265
So when we do our comparison
against DrugBank database

00:39:21.265 --> 00:39:26.100
of known drug targets,
we get ROC AUC of 0.72.

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:30.090
And when we look for human
targets in another database

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:32.450
called Stitch, which
is both manually

00:39:32.450 --> 00:39:36.020
curated and automatically
derived from drug targets,

00:39:36.020 --> 00:39:37.284
we do slightly better.

00:39:37.284 --> 00:39:38.950
And we do the best
for when we're trying

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:41.610
to identify mice targets.

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:44.920
But our success varies
depending on the receptor

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:46.400
type or the target type.

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.070
 

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:53.360
So one of the interesting
findings is that we can,

00:39:53.360 --> 00:39:55.530
by doing this, we
can also pinpoint

00:39:55.530 --> 00:39:58.995
multiple physiological
targets of these drugs.

00:39:58.995 --> 00:40:00.830
So for instance for
diclofenac, which

00:40:00.830 --> 00:40:05.300
is a non-steroidal
agent-- it's used

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:08.840
to treat pain, osteoarthritis,
and rheumatoid arthritis-- what

00:40:08.840 --> 00:40:11.680
we find is that 46% of
the effects of the drug

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:15.390
can be explained by COX-2
knockout, which has features

00:40:15.390 --> 00:40:18.950
like inflammation, gastritis,
constipation, upper GI tract

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:19.680
pain.

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:24.400
And the other roughly 50% can
be explained by a knockout

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:26.900
to PPARg.

00:40:26.900 --> 00:40:30.350
And this regulates metabolism,
proliferation, inflammation,

00:40:30.350 --> 00:40:32.700
and differentiation.

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:35.520
So these genes, when
they're knocked out,

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:38.100
they give you-- and
this is the whole basis

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:40.880
for a lot of biology,
which is this perturbation

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:43.270
to uncover gene
function-- they tell you

00:40:43.270 --> 00:40:45.640
what roles that they're
participating in.

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:49.170
And we can see that in terms
of when the drugs are also

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:50.480
inhibiting those genes.

00:40:50.480 --> 00:40:53.550
 

00:40:53.550 --> 00:41:00.540
OK, so, in summary, I know
that this was a quick run

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:01.990
through ontology.

00:41:01.990 --> 00:41:06.050
Obviously, there is much,
much more to ontology.

00:41:06.050 --> 00:41:10.160
But I think, here, the hope
is to describe to you in a way

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:13.300
that hopefully, you get some
sense of the different tensions

00:41:13.300 --> 00:41:18.350
that we have in building
ontologies by considering

00:41:18.350 --> 00:41:22.290
the many contributions of
the field of philosophy,

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:25.360
of metaphysics, to understanding
the world and the kinds

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:28.580
of things that exist and
how what relationships

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:29.740
hold between them.

00:41:29.740 --> 00:41:32.240
There are many formal
theories of ontology

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:34.480
that we try to
implement in practice,

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:39.570
using formal languages
like OWL and others.

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:44.170
And primarily, OWL, again, is
one of our de facto languages.

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:46.120
It's extremely powerful.

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:49.910
But it's also very
easy to go wrong.

00:41:49.910 --> 00:41:54.130
And so when you're building
the ontology, nothing--

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:56.220
the only way that you
can find that you made

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:59.570
an error is if your ontologies
were sufficiently expressive.

00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:01.370
You've made an
attempt to, again,

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:05.200
formalize these differentia
between the different classes.

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:07.710
And then the reasoner will
do a lot of hard work for you

00:42:07.710 --> 00:42:12.510
to try to find inconsistencies
in those descriptions.

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:14.170
What I hope I've
convinced you is

00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:17.930
that ontologies have a wide
variety of biomedical uses,

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:20.590
from basic answering
of questions--

00:42:20.590 --> 00:42:25.440
this is one of big motivations
from, again, the shared

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:27.300
understanding of
these different terms,

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:30.350
that if you ask questions you
get answers for them in the way

00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:31.700
that you expected them.

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:33.250
But then also that
they have a role

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:37.830
to play in data integration
and in a variety

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:41.540
of biomedical knowledge
discovery settings.

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:46.020
OK, so I want to acknowledge
a number of individuals

00:42:46.020 --> 00:42:51.050
I have shamelessly stolen and
adapted slides from, leaders

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:55.170
in the field, including Mark
Musen, who is here at Stanford;

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:58.740
Barry Smith, who's at the
University of Buffalo,

00:42:58.740 --> 00:43:01.930
a preeminent philosopher in the
field of biomedical ontology,

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:07.180
in particular; Ian Horrocks, one
of the architects for the web

00:43:07.180 --> 00:43:10.660
ontology language; Robert
Hoehndorf, a collaborator

00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:13.070
of mine, where we do
this drug discovery work

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:15.650
using ontologies;
Melissa Haendel, who

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:21.480
is working on integration of
genotype-phenotype descriptions

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:24.410
across species in her
monarch initiative;

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:26.610
and Paul Scofield, who
has also been contributing

00:43:26.610 --> 00:43:29.760
to that and many other things.

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:31.909
All right, and with that,
I'd like to thank you

00:43:31.909 --> 00:43:32.700
for your attention.

00:43:32.700 --> 00:43:34.230
And I'm happy to
take any questions.

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:37.230
 

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:39.940
Mark Musen: Thanks, Michel,
that was fantastic.

00:43:39.940 --> 00:43:41.690
Again, everyone
who has questions

00:43:41.690 --> 00:43:44.000
should type their
questions into the chat.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:46.065
With this many
people on the call,

00:43:46.065 --> 00:43:48.560
if you would unmute everyone,
the background noise

00:43:48.560 --> 00:43:49.310
would be terrible.

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:52.500
So please put your
questions in the chat box.

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:55.330
And we'll try to
get them in order.

00:43:55.330 --> 00:43:57.960
While people are typing, let
me ask you a question, Michel.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:02.200
One of the tensions that we
feel in the big data community

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:05.940
is a question about whether
ontologies are even worth it.

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:09.540
That means there's
this undercurrent

00:44:09.540 --> 00:44:11.399
that ontologies are large.

00:44:11.399 --> 00:44:12.315
They're hard to build.

00:44:12.315 --> 00:44:14.360
They're hard to maintain.

00:44:14.360 --> 00:44:16.550
Can't we just learn
all of the distinctions

00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:17.940
that we need from data?

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:21.920
And is it possible that
the advent of the big data

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:24.595
revolution will make
ontologies less important?

00:44:24.595 --> 00:44:27.180
I'm curious what thoughts
you might have about that.

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.790
Michel Dumontier: Yeah, that's a really
good question, Mark.

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:33.000
There have been-- there are
increasing numbers of attempts

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:36.090
to build data-driven ontology.

00:44:36.090 --> 00:44:39.380
Trey Ideker published a paper, I
think, a couple years ago now--

00:44:39.380 --> 00:44:43.670
I think it was called "Nexo"--
where he was trying to learn

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:47.310
the cellular compartments, or
basically the part of the gene

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:52.240
ontology dealing with cellular
components from interaction

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:53.480
data.

00:44:53.480 --> 00:44:56.360
So in some sense, some
of the relationships

00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:59.560
that we see in ontologies,
the things that we know,

00:44:59.560 --> 00:45:01.370
and the relations
that hold to them

00:45:01.370 --> 00:45:05.520
should be evidenced in the
data that we are collecting.

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:11.130
The problem is, of course, that
what we often use ontologies

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.630
for is this communication
between individuals

00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:16.470
about the terms that they
use and what they mean.

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:20.560
And you know, frankly, science
is constantly changing, right?

00:45:20.560 --> 00:45:23.060
Our understanding of the
basic kinds of things

00:45:23.060 --> 00:45:25.720
and what they do
is always changing.

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:27.860
Annotations of
proteins, for instance,

00:45:27.860 --> 00:45:30.740
functional descriptions of
proteins are being added

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:33.300
and also retracted.

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:36.010
And they need to be
evidenced by experiments.

00:45:36.010 --> 00:45:39.700
And it seems to
me that the value

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:44.160
of having, again, a
consistent set of terms

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:48.500
to use for annotation--
which fundamentally enables

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:50.720
the kinds of
computations that we do--

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:53.810
I don't think the people
who only, for instance,

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:59.190
use text-mined, compound
nouns or anything like that

00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:01.590
would be able to find
the same kinds of things

00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:04.710
that we do when we have
these structured annotations.

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:07.370
Text mining itself
is hugely enhanced

00:46:07.370 --> 00:46:11.050
by having terminologies to use.

00:46:11.050 --> 00:46:16.080
And that includes capturing all
the different kinds of synonyms

00:46:16.080 --> 00:46:17.970
in various languages.

00:46:17.970 --> 00:46:21.320
So it seems like we
will always need,

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:23.950
at least for the foreseeable
future, an involvement

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:26.800
of humans to ensure the
accuracy of the kinds of things

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:28.830
that we're interested
in, and particularly

00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:31.690
to provide this
query-answering capability.

00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:37.060
Of course, I'm
shown to be, like--

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:40.190
even that is being challenged
by systems like Watson that

00:46:40.190 --> 00:46:43.070
are capable of
learning associations

00:46:43.070 --> 00:46:46.210
between different terms and
to answer simple questions

00:46:46.210 --> 00:46:50.630
or questions like on the
Jeopardy challenge that we saw.

00:46:50.630 --> 00:46:53.960
But even Watson uses
structured terminologies.

00:46:53.960 --> 00:46:58.880
So it seems like-- it's
some combination thereof.

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:01.980
And I think those are going to
be the most successful projects

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:05.200
are going to think a little bit
about how far we go with data?

00:47:05.200 --> 00:47:08.450
And then, do we need some
extra input from humans

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:10.910
because the data just
isn't sufficient to do

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:13.850
a task that we're looking for?

00:47:13.850 --> 00:47:15.820
You raise an interesting
issue, Michel.

00:47:15.820 --> 00:47:17.270
And that is, science changes.

00:47:17.270 --> 00:47:21.760
And suddenly you learn that
you have reverse transcriptase

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:23.700
and you make DNA from RNA.

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:27.760
Or sometimes you find out that
Pluto is no longer a planet.

00:47:27.760 --> 00:47:29.640
Given the fact that
the world changes,

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:32.350
how you know whether your
ontologies are actually

00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:33.240
still valid?

00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:37.560
You mentioned that SNOMED
has more than 300,000 terms.

00:47:37.560 --> 00:47:40.870
How do you know
where the bugs are?

00:47:40.870 --> 00:47:45.400
Yeah, that's, I think, where
ontology meets some software

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:46.530
engineering, right?

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:49.000
I think we see--
we're increasingly

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:52.150
seeing-- and we use this in
our own ontology development--

00:47:52.150 --> 00:47:56.560
these patterns where we can
instantiate class descriptions

00:47:56.560 --> 00:47:58.620
in some regular way.

00:47:58.620 --> 00:48:00.790
And by doing that,
we know that we

00:48:00.790 --> 00:48:05.470
can check the consistency of the
knowledge that we're authoring.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:07.815
And I think that scales
to really big ontologies.

00:48:07.815 --> 00:48:11.550
It used to be, 10
years ago, enormously

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:14.600
challenging to reason
about a very large ontology

00:48:14.600 --> 00:48:15.700
like SNOMED.

00:48:15.700 --> 00:48:19.940
And today, you can do it
in extremely short periods

00:48:19.940 --> 00:48:20.440
of time.

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:23.120
It's entirely feasible to do.

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:25.370
The problem, I think,
the bigger problem

00:48:25.370 --> 00:48:28.610
is the fact that we have
500 ontologies, right?

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:30.480
There's so many to choose from.

00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:32.720
They are partially overlapping.

00:48:32.720 --> 00:48:36.270
They are also possibly complete
with the domain because

00:48:36.270 --> 00:48:39.310
of the changes, right,
or our understanding.

00:48:39.310 --> 00:48:43.330
And so if you start
using one ontology

00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:49.490
and another one comes in that is
potentially applicable for you,

00:48:49.490 --> 00:48:50.740
how do you make sense of that?

00:48:50.740 --> 00:48:53.100
How do you use
multiple ontologies

00:48:53.100 --> 00:48:58.190
and not confuse everybody about
what it is that you're doing

00:48:58.190 --> 00:49:01.370
or how to use this in
some computational way?

00:49:01.370 --> 00:49:04.760
So I think this is one of
the next great challenges--

00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:09.120
is making sense of the 500-plus
ontologies in BioPortal

00:49:09.120 --> 00:49:12.120
in a way that users that are
trying to do their task, which,

00:49:12.120 --> 00:49:14.320
let's say, is
annotation or analysis

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:16.370
are pretty much, like,
they don't need to worry

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:18.260
about all this other stuff.

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:20.630
But getting to that
point, I think,

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:25.032
is-- still poses a significant
challenge for all of us.

00:49:25.032 --> 00:49:26.310
Mark Musen: I agree.

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:29.485
A questioner is asking, "How
do developers of ontologies

00:49:29.485 --> 00:49:32.492
get feedback from the
communities that use them?

00:49:32.492 --> 00:49:35.410
And there any other-- any
kinds of standards or tools

00:49:35.410 --> 00:49:38.740
that can facilitate
that kind of feedback?"

00:49:38.740 --> 00:49:43.130
My understanding is-- so
if you go to BioPortal,

00:49:43.130 --> 00:49:46.250
for instance, is that
you'll see metadata

00:49:46.250 --> 00:49:48.980
about the ontologies,
which includes-- often

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:51.050
includes the home
page for the ontology.

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:54.240
But more and more of these
ontology-based efforts

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:59.290
have some kind of mailing list
or discussion forum as well

00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:00.580
as issue trackers.

00:50:00.580 --> 00:50:03.500
This is, some of these
things are, for instance,

00:50:03.500 --> 00:50:06.400
foundational principles in
the Open Biomedical Ontology

00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:08.500
community.

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:11.430
People can make
suggestions for new terms.

00:50:11.430 --> 00:50:14.850
They can suggest
revisions, and so on.

00:50:14.850 --> 00:50:16.840
However, again,
there is no guarantee

00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:18.900
that your terms that
you're suggesting

00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:20.830
are going to be incorporated.

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:24.150
People have vigorous debates
about the definition of things.

00:50:24.150 --> 00:50:26.430
And this certainly happens
in health care terminology

00:50:26.430 --> 00:50:31.560
setting, whether it's
ICD or SNOMED or whatnot.

00:50:31.560 --> 00:50:34.260
This pits people
against each other,

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:36.800
trying to figure out
what exactly are we

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:39.700
talking about here,
and which-- if we

00:50:39.700 --> 00:50:43.860
do this is, is it pragmatic for
the users of these ontologies?

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:45.950
So that tension is always there.

00:50:45.950 --> 00:50:48.810
But I think the biggest
thing, and something

00:50:48.810 --> 00:50:52.690
that I'd encourage everybody,
is that when you see something

00:50:52.690 --> 00:50:57.020
not quite working the way that
you had hoped, that, I think,

00:50:57.020 --> 00:51:00.350
at this point, we're sort
of beyond the bonanza,

00:51:00.350 --> 00:51:03.800
the wild west of ontology
building with so many now.

00:51:03.800 --> 00:51:07.160
Now, it's really going to be
about refinement and hopefully,

00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:10.670
some consolidation,
right, some ability for us

00:51:10.670 --> 00:51:14.990
to have a coherent view
of all of these ontologies

00:51:14.990 --> 00:51:19.334
that is maximally
useful for all users.

00:51:19.334 --> 00:51:20.750
Mark Musen: A question points
out that there's

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:22.866
another ontology
language called OBOE,

00:51:22.866 --> 00:51:24.990
and wonders how that's
different from OWL,

00:51:25.060 --> 00:51:29.420
and why is there more than one
language to build ontologies?

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:35.700
Michel Dumontier: Yeah, so OBOE-- one of the early benefits of OBOE--

00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:37.250
it's a very simple
language, again,

00:51:37.250 --> 00:51:41.390
to capture these basic
definitions of-- so

00:51:41.390 --> 00:51:43.090
human-readable
labels, definitions,

00:51:43.090 --> 00:51:47.490
synonyms had been extended
to contain, again,

00:51:47.490 --> 00:51:50.730
the ability to do this
hierarchical organization

00:51:50.730 --> 00:51:53.360
and descriptions of the
kinds of relationships.

00:51:53.360 --> 00:51:57.590
And some work-- I think,
maybe seven or seven years ago

00:51:57.590 --> 00:52:02.870
or something-- was to then
map languages like OBOE

00:52:02.870 --> 00:52:05.060
to the web ontology language.

00:52:05.060 --> 00:52:09.390
So there is a formal,
now semantic to OBOE.

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:11.720
It mirrors what we see in OWL.

00:52:11.720 --> 00:52:14.860
And so OBOE is now
a syntax that can

00:52:14.860 --> 00:52:20.640
express the OWL axioms that you
might generate, and vice versa.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:23.970
 

00:52:23.970 --> 00:52:27.060
Again, from the perspective
of language development,

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:28.310
there are many languages.

00:52:28.310 --> 00:52:30.720
Of course, I only
touched on one.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:34.352
There are many more
expressive languages than OWL.

00:52:34.352 --> 00:52:35.810
There are lots of
reasons to choose

00:52:35.810 --> 00:52:38.660
a different kind of language.

00:52:38.660 --> 00:52:44.740
But frankly, this is a
standard from the W3C.

00:52:44.740 --> 00:52:50.890
And just like how HTML provided
a generic platform for us

00:52:50.890 --> 00:52:53.840
to read web pages with
a single browser--

00:52:53.840 --> 00:52:56.820
we can browse the
whole internet,

00:52:56.820 --> 00:53:00.370
or a very large part of
the internet using that.

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:04.190
What we don't want
to see is languages

00:53:04.190 --> 00:53:06.170
that don't interoperate
with one another.

00:53:06.170 --> 00:53:10.160
So what I get the sense
of is that new languages

00:53:10.160 --> 00:53:12.320
or new developments
are building on

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:14.350
these formally
defined semantics,

00:53:14.350 --> 00:53:17.790
providing the correspondence
between different languages.

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:21.070
And then, in that way,
we extend our capacity

00:53:21.070 --> 00:53:23.555
to express different
kinds of constructs.

00:53:23.555 --> 00:53:26.370
 

00:53:26.370 --> 00:53:29.060
Mark Musen: Is there a standard
ontology database

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:31.050
endorsed by NIH that
allows conversion

00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:32.790
between disparate resources?

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:34.840
And would BioPortal
eventually be it?

00:53:34.840 --> 00:53:37.390
 

00:53:37.390 --> 00:53:38.640
Michel Dumontier: Yeah, I don't know, Mark.

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:40.180
Is it?

00:53:40.180 --> 00:53:41.950
Mark Musen: I don't know.

00:53:41.950 --> 00:53:44.630
I don't think NIH
necessarily goes

00:53:44.630 --> 00:53:45.935
around endorsing standards.

00:53:45.935 --> 00:53:47.435
But I think BioPortal
would probably

00:53:47.435 --> 00:53:51.320
be it, inasmuch as it
is the resource that

00:53:51.320 --> 00:53:53.900
contains all the publicly
available ontologies

00:53:53.900 --> 00:53:55.420
and biomedicine [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:55.420 --> 00:53:57.960
Other resources tend
to be more selective.

00:53:57.960 --> 00:53:59.820
So in some sense,
I think, BioPortal

00:53:59.820 --> 00:54:01.500
might be that resource.

00:54:01.500 --> 00:54:04.840
But I think it's really
up to what users need

00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:07.450
and what their
applications require.

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:12.150
Michel Dumontier: Yeah, I mean, there are
other ontology repositories.

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:14.130
The European
Bioinformatics Institute

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:17.190
have an ontology repository.

00:54:17.190 --> 00:54:21.090
There is this [INAUDIBLE]
ontology repository,

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:23.790
the AberOWL ontology repository.

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:26.820
And then there's a variety
of ontology repositories

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:30.380
that go beyond the biomedical
scope, like the Tones one,

00:54:30.380 --> 00:54:31.090
and so on.

00:54:31.090 --> 00:54:33.790
So there is a smattering
of different ontology

00:54:33.790 --> 00:54:35.920
repositories.

00:54:35.920 --> 00:54:39.160
Many of them are drawing
from the source code.

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:42.170
Like repositories where
these ontologies are stored,

00:54:42.170 --> 00:54:50.260
they offer some kind of both
archival and/or change-view

00:54:50.260 --> 00:54:52.020
functionality.

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:56.140
So I think it's just a
matter of looking at them

00:54:56.140 --> 00:54:58.730
and seeing what works for you.

00:54:58.730 --> 00:55:02.840
Mark Musen: OK, are many people
conversant or skilled

00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:05.490
in using multiple ontologies?

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:08.170
I'm not sure if that's a
question about the workforce

00:55:08.170 --> 00:55:14.260
or a training question or what's
necessary to actually conduct

00:55:14.260 --> 00:55:15.870
work in this area?

00:55:15.870 --> 00:55:19.120
Is there much work to be done in
the mapping between ontologies?

00:55:19.120 --> 00:55:21.470
Maybe those are two questions.

00:55:21.470 --> 00:55:24.490
Michel Dumontier: OK, so the first
question pertains

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:29.880
to-- so is it is it common for
us to use multiple ontologies?

00:55:29.880 --> 00:55:32.150
Sorry, Mark, is that right?

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:33.230
Mark Musen: Yeah.

00:55:33.230 --> 00:55:34.630
Michel Dumontier: OK.

00:55:34.630 --> 00:55:38.820
Mark Musen: And I guess-- are there
many people who can do it?

00:55:38.820 --> 00:55:41.320
Michel Dumontier: Oh, I know many
people who can do it.

00:55:41.320 --> 00:55:43.750
Mark Musen: In any case, what is the
job market for ontology?

00:55:43.750 --> 00:55:46.720
 

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:51.640
Michel Dumontier: Well clearly, again, the
gene ontology, I think,

00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:55.360
has provided or afforded
so much tool development,

00:55:55.360 --> 00:55:56.720
software development.

00:55:56.720 --> 00:56:00.920
Many bioinformatics
researchers are fundamentally

00:56:00.920 --> 00:56:02.470
familiar with that.

00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:08.000
We can use the gene ontology
for enrichment analysis,

00:56:08.000 --> 00:56:11.472
all kinds of
association analysis,

00:56:11.472 --> 00:56:12.680
all kinds of functions there.

00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:17.050
So there's plenty of tutorials
and code and training material

00:56:17.050 --> 00:56:21.710
as to using an ontology like
the ontology for biological data

00:56:21.710 --> 00:56:22.700
analysis.

00:56:22.700 --> 00:56:25.180
And similarly, I'd argue that
probably in the health care

00:56:25.180 --> 00:56:28.300
domain, there is the
similar kinds of things

00:56:28.300 --> 00:56:32.260
that are being done for cohort
analysis, risk analysis.

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:34.610
Our colleague, Nick [INAUDIBLE],
does this kind of work

00:56:34.610 --> 00:56:38.690
on using ontologies
for processing

00:56:38.690 --> 00:56:40.620
the notes of electronic
health care records,

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:44.200
and subsequently doing
analyses to look at cohorts

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:48.940
and their risks, the
diseases, and so on.

00:56:48.940 --> 00:56:52.380
I think it's mostly a
matter of these use cases.

00:56:52.380 --> 00:56:55.780
In our work, where we look
at multiple properties

00:56:55.780 --> 00:56:59.110
of, for instance, drugs
stemming from the molecular

00:56:59.110 --> 00:57:01.360
to epidemiological,
we're dealing

00:57:01.360 --> 00:57:05.530
with all kinds of
terminologies, upwards

00:57:05.530 --> 00:57:07.420
of 20 different
terminologies for one

00:57:07.420 --> 00:57:12.630
analysis, coupled with 10 to 15
different databases, and so on.

00:57:12.630 --> 00:57:15.900
So it becomes a
pretty big project,

00:57:15.900 --> 00:57:18.370
but increasingly easier to do.

00:57:18.370 --> 00:57:20.639
We offer-- for instance,
another project

00:57:20.640 --> 00:57:22.430
that we do is called
Biotreaty [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:22.430 --> 00:57:25.370
We have data in a
uniform representation

00:57:25.370 --> 00:57:26.950
using semantic web standards.

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:28.880
You couple that
with the ontologies.

00:57:28.880 --> 00:57:30.880
You vastly reduce
the amount of work

00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:33.710
you have to do to
use those data.

00:57:33.710 --> 00:57:35.760
But it's up to you to
think about the application

00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:37.170
that you want to pursue.

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:39.790
 

00:57:39.790 --> 00:57:40.290
Mark Musen: Great.

00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:42.710
What tools are available
to identify ontology terms

00:57:42.710 --> 00:57:43.345
in literature?

00:57:43.345 --> 00:57:46.320
 

00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:50.160
Michel Dumontier: Yeah, well, so I mean,
I can tout here--

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:55.080
so the NCPO, which Mark
leads, has an annotator tool.

00:57:55.080 --> 00:57:57.220
So you can pick and
choose the ontologies

00:57:57.220 --> 00:58:00.840
you want to use to do
an annotation task.

00:58:00.840 --> 00:58:02.310
So that's very useful.

00:58:02.310 --> 00:58:05.850
I know there's a UI
and an API component.

00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:08.230
That's a real quick
and dirty kind

00:58:08.230 --> 00:58:13.180
of way to start analyzing text
that match the terms that you

00:58:13.180 --> 00:58:16.150
have in the ontology space.

00:58:16.150 --> 00:58:19.330
Again, the challenge
is, is really

00:58:19.330 --> 00:58:21.730
worrying about potential
false positives.

00:58:21.730 --> 00:58:24.920
A lot of terms,
especially acronyms,

00:58:24.920 --> 00:58:26.800
have multiple different
kinds of meanings.

00:58:26.800 --> 00:58:28.920
They're not unambiguous.

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:30.970
They map to many
different things.

00:58:30.970 --> 00:58:33.420
So I think the challenge
with-- certainly,

00:58:33.420 --> 00:58:36.230
text-mining with ontologies
is you have to really think

00:58:36.230 --> 00:58:37.230
about your use case.

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:42.680
If you're interested in a
large-scale analysis where

00:58:42.680 --> 00:58:47.470
you're not asking a question
about any individual,

00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:50.520
then you should be able
to find signal over noise,

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:53.380
even if you don't do a
great job of always being

00:58:53.380 --> 00:58:55.260
precise with your annotations.

00:58:55.260 --> 00:59:00.220
Now that doesn't always hold
true for some kinds of diseases

00:59:00.220 --> 00:59:03.190
or procedures that
you're looking at.

00:59:03.190 --> 00:59:05.410
You're using very common words.

00:59:05.410 --> 00:59:08.130
And then it's just
practically impossible

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:13.040
without some other
corresponding information.

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:14.690
There are other tool kits.

00:59:14.690 --> 00:59:21.180
Increasingly we see also aspects
to some kind of deep learning

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:25.720
over text to better extract
or-- either identify

00:59:25.720 --> 00:59:28.590
entities or extrapolations.

00:59:28.590 --> 00:59:30.330
Lots of work is
being done there.

00:59:30.330 --> 00:59:34.140
But again, working with text
is extremely challenging.

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:37.810
And I think there's
plenty of work

00:59:37.810 --> 00:59:42.210
left to be done to improve
the methods for good use

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:43.495
of ontology over text.

00:59:43.495 --> 00:59:46.190
 

00:59:46.190 --> 00:59:48.090
Mark Musen: What do you do with
a situation when

00:59:48.090 --> 00:59:50.510
the ontology has to
categorize an entity

00:59:50.510 --> 00:59:53.040
and it doesn't fit cleanly?

00:59:53.040 --> 00:59:55.550
A questioner asks, what
you do with aspirin, when

00:59:55.550 --> 00:59:58.800
in one context, it's a pain
reliever, in another context,

00:59:58.800 --> 01:00:00.710
it's a blood thinner?

01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:02.557
How do you answer
simple questions like,

01:00:02.557 --> 01:00:03.390
what is this entity?

01:00:03.390 --> 01:00:05.190
How do you classify it?

01:00:05.190 --> 01:00:06.690
Michel Dumontier: Yeah, that's a
really good question.

01:00:06.690 --> 01:00:09.460
It's actually one that we're
struggling with right now as we

01:00:09.460 --> 01:00:13.550
look-- as we use-- we try
to do prediction of new drug

01:00:13.550 --> 01:00:14.910
indications.

01:00:14.910 --> 01:00:16.940
And a lot of these
indications are

01:00:16.940 --> 01:00:21.090
drawn from actually text-mined
annotations, in which there

01:00:21.090 --> 01:00:22.960
are many incorrect values.

01:00:22.960 --> 01:00:29.440
 

01:00:29.440 --> 01:00:31.430
It's a very difficult problem.

01:00:31.430 --> 01:00:35.940
I think our approach is mostly
that you can create structures

01:00:35.940 --> 01:00:37.630
or knowledge structures
that capture,

01:00:37.630 --> 01:00:43.570
for instance, a context in which
some function or functionality

01:00:43.570 --> 01:00:45.150
is being executed.

01:00:45.150 --> 01:00:47.890
So again, if you want to
articulate that aspirin

01:00:47.890 --> 01:00:52.180
can act as a potential blood
thinner in some circumstance,

01:00:52.180 --> 01:00:55.500
then you really need the
ontology, or the description,

01:00:55.500 --> 01:00:56.530
to do that.

01:00:56.530 --> 01:00:59.490
We have developed a
tool called [? HighQ, ?]

01:00:59.490 --> 01:01:02.950
where we have this event-based
representation, where we say,

01:01:02.950 --> 01:01:05.730
OK, what are the
components in the event?

01:01:05.730 --> 01:01:09.310
What are the agents that, for
instance, potentially cause

01:01:09.310 --> 01:01:09.920
change?

01:01:09.920 --> 01:01:11.720
And what's the
biological context?

01:01:11.720 --> 01:01:15.520
Or what's the circumstance
under which this occurs?

01:01:15.520 --> 01:01:19.680
And so this kind of approach
is event-based representation

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:23.170
is also very prominent in
other kinds of analyses,

01:01:23.170 --> 01:01:27.340
analytical analyses, but is not
really prevalent in the way we

01:01:27.340 --> 01:01:29.240
currently construct ontologies.

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:33.720
There's this modulation of
the context, whether in space

01:01:33.720 --> 01:01:37.160
or in time or other
interactions with other systems

01:01:37.160 --> 01:01:40.110
is not often done.

01:01:40.110 --> 01:01:42.880
But increasingly,
I think, that work

01:01:42.880 --> 01:01:46.065
that [INAUDIBLE],
Chris [INAUDIBLE],

01:01:46.065 --> 01:01:49.060
Melissa Haendel, Peter
Robinson, and others are doing

01:01:49.060 --> 01:01:51.990
are also striving to capture
this sort of information.

01:01:51.990 --> 01:01:53.740
So it's a little
bit of provenance,

01:01:53.740 --> 01:01:56.550
a little bit of context,
and, I think, an enhancement

01:01:56.550 --> 01:02:01.520
of the description of these
functional circumstances that

01:02:01.520 --> 01:02:02.340
need to happen.

01:02:02.340 --> 01:02:03.710
So we do it in our work.

01:02:03.710 --> 01:02:06.800
But it's not prevalent,
I think, in doing this.

01:02:06.800 --> 01:02:10.350
Even when you look at UniProt
that captures these-- again,

01:02:10.350 --> 01:02:17.450
these annotations of, like, I
showed you for RAC1A functions,

01:02:17.450 --> 01:02:22.060
this kind of control only may
happen under some circumstances

01:02:22.060 --> 01:02:23.000
and not others.

01:02:23.000 --> 01:02:24.870
And that's not
necessarily detailed.

01:02:24.870 --> 01:02:27.740
What they'll tell you is what's
the experimental evidence

01:02:27.740 --> 01:02:29.600
for the annotation
that you see, but not

01:02:29.600 --> 01:02:33.090
necessarily the full context
that you need to unambiguously

01:02:33.090 --> 01:02:38.170
interpret or understand and
what context that's occurring.

01:02:38.170 --> 01:02:38.910
Mark Musen: Thanks, Michel.

01:02:38.910 --> 01:02:40.874
We've reached the
end of the hour.

01:02:40.874 --> 01:02:42.290
The questions have
been fantastic.

01:02:42.290 --> 01:02:43.789
We actually have
many more questions

01:02:43.789 --> 01:02:44.751
that we didn't get to.

01:02:44.751 --> 01:02:46.500
But unfortunately, we
don't have the time.

01:02:46.500 --> 01:02:48.690
Let me thank you for
your presentation, which

01:02:48.690 --> 01:02:49.600
was wonderful.

01:02:49.600 --> 01:02:51.370
And we thank everybody
who dialed in

01:02:51.370 --> 01:02:55.700
and everybody who will watch
the replay of your talk online.

01:02:55.700 --> 01:02:58.074
And we'll have another
session next week.

01:02:58.074 --> 01:02:58.740
Thanks everyone.

01:02:58.740 --> 01:02:59.240
Michel Dumontier: Thank you.

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:00.490
Mark Musen: Bye-bye.

01:03:00.490 --> 01:03:02.040
Michel Dumontier: Bye.

